# The ten themes: passage (Cicero register), tension, coverage, two cases at opposite poles.
THEMES = [
dict(id="power", title="Man and Power", tension=["obedience", "consent"],
 passage="Every society must decide who commands and persuade the rest to obey, and the persuading has always been the harder part. Kings pointed to God, republics to the people, dictators to the emergency; each claim held exactly as long as it was believed, and belief has failed before fear every time. Power is cheap to seize and expensive to keep, and the price is paid in legitimacy, a currency no regime has ever been able to print.",
 covers="consent, inheritance, charisma, force; democracy and despotism as traffic between them; revolution",
 cases=[dict(pole="obedience", case="China's surveillance state"), dict(pole="consent", case="Taiwan's democracy, from the same civilisation")]),
dict(id="god", title="Man and God", tension=["the sacred as the state's foundation", "the sacred as its rival"],
 passage="Faith has built more states than it has broken and broken only those that tried to own it. Men have killed for a doctrine, died for it, and then, exhausted, learned to tolerate what they could not convert; the toleration came from fatigue first and philosophy later. Every century since has announced the end of religion in politics, and religion has not read the announcement.",
 covers="Reformation, toleration, holy war, secularisation, religion's return to politics",
 cases=[dict(pole="foundation", case="Iran since 1979"), dict(pole="rival", case="Turkey under Atatürk's settlement and its erosion")]),
dict(id="war", title="Man and War", tension=["security through strength", "security through restraint"],
 passage="War is the oldest of institutions and the one most often pronounced dead. Men fight, as Thucydides wrote, out of fear, honour and interest, and the balance among the three decides whether the war is short or ruinous. The peace that follows is the most consequential act of any war, and it is almost always made by men too tired to make it well.",
 covers="the international system, alliances, deterrence, settlements",
 cases=[dict(pole="strength", case="Israel's doctrine"), dict(pole="restraint", case="Japan's Article 9")]),
dict(id="scarcity", title="Man and Scarcity", tension=["plenty through markets", "justice through distribution"],
 passage="There has never been enough, and every economic system is a proposal about who should go without. Markets, plans, guilds, empires and rationing are all answers to the same arithmetic, and each has starved someone. The discovery of the modern age is the darkest one: that men die of hunger beside full granaries when no one is permitted to say that they are dying.",
 covers="famine amid plenty, capitalism and its rivals, debt, one commodity becoming a nation's politics",
 cases=[dict(pole="markets", case="Norway's oil fund"), dict(pole="distribution", case="Venezuela's oil state")]),
dict(id="machine", title="Man and Machine", tension=["mastery", "dependence"],
 passage="Man makes his tools, and then his tools make the world he must live in. Print made the Reformation possible and the nation-state inevitable; steam built the factory, the city and the colony; the bomb gave the great powers a peace founded on terror; the network has assembled a crowd that never goes home. Every machine has arrived as a servant, and none has stayed one.",
 covers="print, steam, the bomb, the network",
 cases=[dict(pole="mastery", case="the Green Revolution's defeat of famine"), dict(pole="dependence", case="the 2021 Texas grid failure")]),
dict(id="nature", title="Man and Nature", tension=["mastery", "dependence"],
 passage="Climate, plague and distance governed human affairs long before anyone thought to record it, and they govern still. The crops and pathogens that crossed the Atlantic after 1492 moved more people and killed more than any conqueror in history. Nature is the slowest actor on the stage and the one that is always still there when the others have left.",
 covers="the Columbian exchange, plague, the little ice age, coal, the warming century",
 cases=[dict(pole="mastery", case="the United Arab Emirates"), dict(pole="dependence", case="Darfur")]),
dict(id="stranger", title="Man and the Stranger", tension=["the circle widening", "the circle closing"],
 passage="Every people has drawn a line between itself and the others, by blood, faith, tongue, colour or paper, and the line has been redrawn more often by lawyers than by soldiers. Who may belong decides who may own, vote, move, marry and live. The twentieth century widened the circle further than any age before it and, in the same decades, closed it with a thoroughness no age had managed.",
 covers="nation, race, slavery, migration, citizenship",
 cases=[dict(pole="widening", case="South Africa's 1996 constitution"), dict(pole="closing", case="Myanmar's expulsion of the Rohingya")]),
dict(id="truth", title="Man and Truth", tension=["authority", "inquiry"],
 passage="Every age has appointed a keeper of truth, church, crown, academy or press, and every age has lived through the day the keeper lost the keys. Gutenberg, Galileo, Darwin and the algorithm each broke a monopoly on what may be known, and in every case the breaking shook the state more than the knowledge did. Who is permitted to say a thing is true has mattered as much as whether it is.",
 covers="Galileo, the press, Darwin, propaganda, the laboratory, the feed",
 cases=[dict(pole="authority", case="the Great Firewall"), dict(pole="inquiry", case="Wikipedia")]),
dict(id="time", title="Man and Time", tension=["progress", "cycle"],
 passage="Men have believed that history climbs, that it falls, and that it turns in a circle, and each belief has fitted the facts for about a century before the facts moved. Whether history is going anywhere is the question beneath all the others in this atlas, and the atlas leaves it open, showing which claims about the direction of things have survived five hundred years of evidence and which have failed.",
 covers="Ibn Khaldun's dynasties, the Enlightenment's arrow, the twentieth century's doubt",
 cases=[dict(pole="progress", case="the end of history, 1989"), dict(pole="cycle", case="the return of great-power war, 2022")]),
dict(id="memory", title="Man and Memory", tension=["the duty to remember", "the need to forget"],
 passage="A people is made by what it chooses to remember and unmade by what it cannot forget. Nations build more monuments to their defeats than to their victories, because a defeat can be owned by everyone; and how a country tells the story of its worst day is the most reliable guide to what it will do on its next one.",
 covers="monuments and their removal, the Lost Cause, Nanjing, the Holocaust, the Nakba, chosen ancestors",
 cases=[dict(pole="remember", case="Germany's Holocaust memorial"), dict(pole="forget", case="Spain's pact of forgetting and its reversal in 2007")]),
]
